<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-buoyancy" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Buoyancy</title>

    <p>
        <term>Weight</term> is the gravity force experienced by an object due to its mass <m>m</m> [kg].
        The magnitude of weight <m>W</m> [N] is given by
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            W = mg
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>g</m> [<m>\text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2}</m>] is the gravity acceleration.
    </p>

    <p>
        The definition of the buoyancy force follows from <term>Archimedes' Principle</term>,
        which states that the upward force exerted on an object immersed in a fluid by the surrounding fluid
        equals the magnitude of the weight of the fluid the object has displaced.
        The magnitude of the weight of the displaced fluid <m>W_{fluid}</m> is
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-weight-displaced-fluid-start" number="yes">
            W_{fluid} = m_{fluid} \, g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>m_{fluid}</m> is the mass of the displaced fluid.
        If a volume <m>V</m> [<m>\text{m}^{3}</m>] of fluid
        with constant density <m>\rho_{fluid}</m> [<m>\text{kg} \, \text{m}^{-3}</m>]
        is displaced by an object with volume <m>V</m>,
        <m>m_{fluid} = \rho_{fluid} \, V</m>
        and <xref ref="eqn-weight-displaced-fluid-start" text="type-global" /> becomes
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-weight-displaced-fluid" number="yes">
            W_{fluid} = \rho_{fluid} \, V g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-weight-displaced-fluid" text="type-global" /> gives the upward force the immersed object experiences.
    </p>

    <p>
        The immersed object also experiences the downward force of gravity as expressed through its own weight <m>W_{object}</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-weight-object-start" number="yes">
            W_{object} = -m_{object} \, g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>m_{object}</m> is the mass of the object.
        As noted above,
        the object has the same volume <m>V</m> as the fluid it displaces.
        If the object has constant density <m>\rho_{object}</m>,
        <m>m_{object} = \rho_{object} \, V</m>
        and <xref ref="eqn-weight-object-start" text="type-global" /> becomes
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-weight-object" number="yes">
            W_{object} = -\rho_{object} \, V g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        The <term>buoyancy force</term> is the sum of these vertically-directed forces acting on the immersed object:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                F_B \amp = W_{fluid} + W_{object}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp = \rho_{fluid} \, V g - \rho_{object} \, V g
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-buoyancy-force" number="yes">
                \amp = (\rho_{fluid} - \rho_{object}) \, V g
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        It follows from <xref ref="eqn-buoyancy-force" text="type-global" />
        that if the object is less dense than the fluid it displaces
        (i.e., <m>\rho_{object} \lt \rho_{fluid}</m>),
        then the buoyancy force acting on it is directed upward
        (i.e., <m>F_B \gt 0</m>).
        Likewise,
        if the object is denser than the fluid it displaces
        (i.e., <m>\rho_{object} \gt \rho_{fluid}</m>),
        then the buoyancy force acting on it is directed downward
        (i.e., <m>F_B \lt 0</m>).
    </p>

    <p>
        In meteorology,
        we typically take the immersed object to be an air parcel
        and the fluid within which it is immersed to be the air surrounding it,
        i.e., its environment.
        Following the notation of <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>,
        we replace <m>\rho_{fluid} = \rho</m> for the environmental density
        and <m>\rho_{object} = \rho'</m> for the air parcel density.
        Then <xref ref="eqn-buoyancy-force" text="type-global" /> becomes
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-buoyancy-force-meteorology" number="yes">
            F_B = (\rho - \rho') \, V g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Since measuring the masses of air parcels is not feasible,
        it is convenient to deal with forces per unit mass,
        i.e., accelerations.
        It follows that we divide <xref ref="eqn-buoyancy-force-meteorology" text="type-global" />
        by the mass of the air parcel <m>m' = \rho' \, V</m>
        to produce the <term>buoyancy acceleration</term> <m>a_B</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-density" number="yes">
            a_B
            =
            \frac{F_B}{m'}
            =
            \frac{(\rho - \rho') \, V g}{\rho' \, V}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\rho - \rho'}{\rho'}
            \right) g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Assuming the air parcel (1) is perfectly dry
        and (2) has pressure that instantaneously adjusts to the environmental pressure
        (i.e., <m>p' = p</m>, which is a common and accurate assumption under most conditions),
        we can rewrite <xref ref="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-density" text="type-global" />
        using <xref ref="eqn-ideal-gas-law-density" text="type-global" />:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-temperature" number="yes">
            a_B
            =
            \left(
                \frac{T' - T}{T}
            \right) g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>T</m> is the environmental temperature
        and <m>T'</m> is the air parcel temperature.
        Furthermore,
        using the definition of potential temperature given in <xref ref="eqn-potential-temperature" text="type-global" />,
        we can rewrite <xref ref="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-temperature" text="type-global" />
        in terms of <m>\theta</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-potential-temperature" number="yes">
            a_B
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\theta' - \theta}{\theta}
            \right) g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>\theta</m> is the environmental potential temperature
        and <m>\theta'</m> is the air parcel potential temperature.
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref first="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-density" last="eqn-buoyancy-acceleration-potential-temperature"/>
        lead to the following three cases,
        which are depicted in <xref ref="ch7-buoyancy" text="type-global" /> below.
    </p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When a perfectly dry air parcel is denser and therefore colder and potentially colder than its environment
                    (i.e., <m>\rho' \gt \rho</m>, <m>T' \lt T</m>, and <m>\theta' \lt \theta</m>),
                    the air parcel is <term>negatively buoyant</term>.
                    In other words, <m>F_B</m> and <m>a_B</m> are directed downward (i.e., are negative).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When a perfectly dry air parcel is less dense and therefore warmer and potentially warmer than its environment
                    (i.e., <m>\rho' \lt \rho</m>, <m>T' \gt T</m>, and <m>\theta' \gt \theta</m>),
                    the air parcel is <term>positively buoyant</term>.
                    In other words, <m>F_B</m> and <m>a_B</m> are directed upward (i.e., are positive).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When a perfectly dry air parcel has the same density and therefore the same temperature and potential temperature as its environment
                    (i.e., <m>\rho' = \rho</m>, <m>T' = T</m>, and <m>\theta' = \theta</m>),
                    the air parcel is <term>neutrally buoyant</term>.
                    In other words, <m>F_B</m> and <m>a_B</m> are 0.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch7-buoyancy">
        <caption>
            An air parcel with edges parallel to the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-, and <m>z</m>-axes of the local Cartesian coordinate system,
            shown for cases of negative buoyancy (left), positive buoyancy (middle), and neutral buoyancy (right),
            where the vector labeled <m>\vec{F}_B</m> represents the buoyancy force acting on the air parcel.
            <m>\vec{F}_B</m> is directed downward for the case of negative buoyancy,
            upward for the case of positive buoyancy,
            and is absent for the case of neutral buoyancy.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch7-buoyancy.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Rectangular prism with length, width, and height
                and vector representing the buoyancy force
                pulling downward on the prism (left)
                and upward on the prism (middle).
                The vector is absent for the prism on the right.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

</subsection>